believed Albert Wesker had. An artificial optimiser
that would watch Crane's every decision,

4704
06:26:46,412 --> 06:26:52,681
reward the good ones, and — somehow, from the sum of
all human knowledge in Lumen's servers

4705
06:26:52,701 --> 06:26:55,791
— hand him capabilities that
nobody else had.

4706
06:26:56,361 --> 06:27:02,262
It did not work. It could not work. It produced very
good advice, and several patents,

4707
06:27:02,282 --> 06:27:08,458
and a great deal of analysis. But it did not produce a
single orphan idea — a single thing

4708
06:27:08,478 --> 06:27:14,310
that had not been, somewhere, in the data it had
learned from. Everything it gave him

4709
06:27:14,330 --> 06:27:15,136
had parents.

4710
06:27:15,706 --> 06:27:21,421
The thing that Albert Wesker had did not come from
the data. It came from outside. And

4711
06:27:21,441 --> 06:27:27,223
Sebastian Crane, who did not believe in anything
that could not be modelled, spent four

4712
06:27:27,243 --> 06:27:32,958
months and nine billion dollars proving to himself
that it could not be modelled — and

4713
06:27:32,978 --> 06:27:35,826
then refused to believe what
he had proved.

4714
06:27:37,895 --> 06:27:43,818
They met in June, on a rooftop in San Francisco,
after a conference at which they had

4715
06:27:43,838 --> 06:27:47,594
both been scheduled to speak and
only one of them had.

4716
06:27:48,164 --> 06:27:53,590
Crane walked straight up to him at the railing,
without introduction, and without small

4717
06:27:53,610 --> 06:27:55,907
talk, which the Director appreciated.

4718
06:27:56,597 --> 06:27:57,900
What's feeding you?

4719
06:27:58,419 --> 06:27:59,274
I'm sorry?

4720
06:27:59,794 --> 06:28:04,244
I've modelled you. I've put everything you've ever
done into the largest predictive system

4721
06:28:04,264 --> 06:28:08,516
on Earth, and it can predict everyone except you. It
says your decisions are optimised

4722
06:28:08,536 --> 06:28:12,936
against an external reward function. It says
sixty-three of your patents have no parents.

4723
06:28:12,956 --> 06:28:16,265
It says something is giving you answers. I
want to know what it is.

4724
06:28:18,284 --> 06:28:24,643
The Director looked at him for a long time. The
Cold-Read gave him Sebastian Crane in

4725
06:28:24,663 --> 06:28:31,548
a single glance, and it was an almost comically simple
face to read. Intelligence, enormous.

4726
06:28:31,568 --> 06:28:37,926
Curiosity, enormous. Ego, larger than both. And
envy — raw, childish, total envy — of

4727
06:28:37,946 --> 06:28:41,453
the kind a boy feels for another
boy's bicycle.

4728
06:28:42,143 --> 06:28:45,046
Ambition, Mister Crane. It's what
feeds all of us.

4729
06:28:45,566 --> 06:28:49,986
Don't. I know it isn't. Oracle knows it isn't. I've
spent nine billion dollars trying

4730
06:28:50,006 --> 06:28:53,119
to build what you have, and I can't,
and I need to know why.

4731
06:28:53,640 --> 06:28:56,686
Then you've already learned the most
important thing about it.

4732
06:28:57,205 --> 06:28:58,060
Which is?

4733
06:28:58,580 --> 06:28:59,509
That it's mine.

4734
06:29:02,029 --> 06:29:08,001
He finished his drink, and set down the glass on the
railing, and walked away. And Sebastian

4735
06:29:08,021 --> 06:29:13,994
Crane stood on the rooftop for a long time, looking at
the lights of the bridge, and decided

4736
06:29:14,014 --> 06:29:19,531
— as men like him always decide, when they are told
