4743
06:29:50,017 --> 06:29:52,558
He used all three on Sebastian Crane.

4744
06:29:54,748 --> 06:29:58,888
Why not simply buy him? Or break him,
like the policeman?

4745
06:29:59,407 --> 06:30:04,672
Because he has a machine that can see the shape of
what we are. Buying him won't turn

4746
06:30:04,692 --> 06:30:10,453
off the machine. Breaking him won't turn it off. The
only way to beat a machine that predicts

4747
06:30:10,473 --> 06:30:12,939
you is to teach it the wrong prediction.

4748
06:30:13,460 --> 06:30:15,634
You intend to lie to it.

4749
06:30:16,154 --> 06:30:22,087
I intend to be honest with it. For six months.
Perfectly, predictably honest. And then,

4750
06:30:22,107 --> 06:30:24,688
once, at the moment that matters, not.

4751
06:30:27,207 --> 06:30:31,679
For six months, the Director made decisions
that Oracle could predict.

4752
06:30:32,248 --> 06:30:38,101
He did it deliberately. Every time he acted, he acted
in a way that fit the pattern Oracle

4753
06:30:38,121 --> 06:30:43,974
had drawn of him — the external reward, the optimiser,
the leap. And Oracle, which learned

4754
06:30:43,994 --> 06:30:49,651
from everything, learned from this. Its predictions
of Umbrella improved. Sixty percent

4755
06:30:49,671 --> 06:30:55,524
accuracy, then seventy, then eighty-five. By December,
Oracle was predicting Albert Wesker

4756
06:30:55,544 --> 06:31:01,266
with ninety-one percent confidence, and Sebastian
Crane was sleeping four hours a night,

4757
06:31:01,286 --> 06:31:02,244
and very happy.

4758
06:31:02,815 --> 06:31:08,748
And in December, Oracle made its biggest prediction
of all. It said, with ninety-seven

4759
06:31:08,768 --> 06:31:15,393
percent confidence, that Umbrella was about to launch a
bid for the world's largest manufacturer

4760
06:31:15,413 --> 06:31:21,554
of the specialised chips used to train artificial
intelligence — a company in Taiwan that

4761
06:31:21,574 --> 06:31:27,577
Lumen depended on for everything. The pattern was
unmistakable. Every move the Director

4762
06:31:27,597 --> 06:31:30,208
had made for six months pointed at it.

4763
06:31:30,779 --> 06:31:32,378
So Crane moved first.

4764
06:31:32,947 --> 06:31:39,858
On the ninth of January, twenty thirty-four, Lumen
announced a two-hundred-and-forty-billion-dollar

4765
06:31:39,878 --> 06:31:46,089
offer for the Taiwanese chip manufacturer. It was the
largest technology deal in history.

4766
06:31:46,109 --> 06:31:52,040
It committed every dollar Lumen had and a great
many it did not. It was designed — as

4767
06:31:52,060 --> 06:31:57,292
Crane told his board, with shining eyes — to
get there before Umbrella did.

4768
06:31:57,861 --> 06:31:59,370
Umbrella never bid.

4769
06:32:01,939 --> 06:32:07,894
Umbrella had never been going to bid. Umbrella had
spent six months making every public

4770
06:32:07,914 --> 06:32:13,868
move that a company about to bid would make, and had
not made a single private one. And

4771
06:32:13,888 --> 06:32:19,705
in the week after Lumen's announcement, when the
governments of Taiwan and the United

4772
06:32:19,725 --> 06:32:25,542
States and China all realised at the same moment
that the company that ran a third of

4773
06:32:25,562 --> 06:32:31,585
the world's cloud was about to own the chips that ran
the rest, the deal became the most

4774
06:32:31,605 --> 06:32:34,676
